'''Koyasu Eikichi''' (3 September 1882 – 12 December 1948) was a [[Sanese Baliscans|Sanese-born Baliscan]] [[W:Labor activism|labor activist]] and later [[Araucaria|Araucarian]] delegate to [[Confederal Congress|Congress]]. Ideologically, his worldview combined [[W:Left-wing politics|left-wing politics]] with [[W:Buddhist socialism|Buddhist social teachings]].

Born in [[Okami|Okami, Okaiken]], then rural farming community, Eikichi and his family immigrated to [[Axarquía]] ([[Araucaria]]) in 1892 when he was aged 11, where his father found work on the [[Trans-Atlántido Railroad]]. The death of his father in a train accident in 1905 influenced his decision to become involved in labor organizing, and Eikichi joined the [[Confederación Autónoma del Trabajo]] (CAT), through which he helped laborers [[W:Voter registration|register to vote]]. 

Following his death in 1948, and on the initiative of local labor organizations, the Council of [[Axarquía]] dedicated a five-block pedestrianized area of [[Barrio Tekano|Barrio Tekano's]] core to his memory, now known as [[Avenida Koyasu Eikichi]].

